# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Generate 50 short expository pieces on adversarial machine learning."""
import os

PIECES = []

def add(title, text):
    PIECES.append((title, text.strip()))

add("Adversarial Examples: The Basic Phenomenon", """An adversarial example is an input that has been altered so slightly that a human observer sees no meaningful difference, yet a trained classifier assigns it a different label, often with high confidence. The canonical demonstration is an image of a panda, correctly classified, to which a perturbation of a few grey levels is added; the result still looks like a panda to us but is confidently called a gibbon. The alteration is not random noise. It is computed with knowledge of the model, typically by following the gradient of the loss with respect to the input, so that the change pushes the decision in a chosen direction while remaining tiny under a chosen metric. Two families are usually distinguished. Untargeted attacks merely require the predicted label to change; targeted attacks require a specific wrong class. Because the perturbation is optimized rather than accidental, it can be far smaller than any noise a camera would add, which is why the phenomenon was surprising when it was first reported in 2013 and 2014. The practical significance is that accuracy measured on clean test data says nothing about behaviour under small deliberate changes. The scientific significance is deeper: it shows that the features a network relies on differ from the features a human uses, so a correct prediction can rest on a brittle cue that is easy to move. Adversarial examples are therefore both a security problem and a probe into what these models actually learn.""")
add("Why the Perturbation Is Invisible", """The invisibility of an adversarial perturbation is a consequence of the geometry of high dimensional inputs, not of any special cleverness in the attack. A colour image with a million pixels can be changed in every channel by a small amount without any single change becoming noticeable, as long as the changes stay below the contrast threshold of the human visual system. What matters for perception is local structure: edges, textures and coherent regions. Perturbations that respect an L infinity budget of eight grey levels, the setting used in much published work, spend their allowance independently across pixels, so they add a faint high frequency film rather than a visible object. Human vision is relatively insensitive to such fine grained, low contrast structure, and it also integrates information over larger receptive fields, which further attenuates it. The classifier, by contrast, reads every channel exactly and can exploit correlations across hundreds of thousands of dimensions at once. Its effective sensitivity to a coordinated change is therefore much higher than ours. Two caveats follow. First, invisibility is not guaranteed by a small norm: an attacker with a generous budget can produce a clearly visible but still highly effective distortion. Second, perceptually grounded metrics such as structural similarity or learned perceptual distances describe the difference far better than a pixel norm, and attacks tuned to fool those metrics are usually larger in pixel terms. Invisibility is thus a property of the chosen distance measure and the chosen human observer, not an intrinsic property of the attack.""")
add("Norm Balls and the Geometry of Perturbations", """Every attack must state how much change it is allowed to make, and the usual answer is a norm ball around the original input. The L infinity norm bounds the largest change to any single pixel or channel, which matches a natural guarantee: no individual value moves more than a stated amount. The L two norm bounds the total energy of the perturbation, so it permits a few large changes as long as everything else stays untouched. The L one norm encourages sparse perturbations, concentrating the change in a few coordinates, and the L zero count simply limits how many values may be altered at all. These choices are not interchangeable. The L infinity ball in a million dimensional space is a hypercube whose corners are extremely far from the centre in Euclidean terms, so an L infinity budget of eight grey levels can correspond to a perturbation that a Euclidean argument would consider large, and vice versa. Attack algorithms differ in which geometry they exploit: a sign based step is optimal for L infinity, while a normalised gradient step is the natural analogue for L two. Defenses inherit the same distinction, since robust training against one norm does not automatically transfer to another. The practical lesson is that a robustness number is meaningless without its norm and its radius. Reporting ninety percent robust accuracy without saying that the adversary was limited to two grey levels in an L two ball describes a much weaker claim than it appears to, and comparisons between papers are only meaningful when the geometry and the budget are identical.""")
add("Unrestricted Adversarial Examples", """Norm constrained attacks are convenient for analysis but they do not capture everything a real attacker can do. Unrestricted adversarial examples change the input in ways that are not small in any pixel norm yet leave the semantic content untouched: a rotation of a few degrees, a change of colour balance, a different but equally realistic texture, or a rendering of the same object under different lighting. Such transformations produce images that a human would call the same object, so they are legitimate adversarial inputs, but they lie far outside a small L infinity ball. The reason they matter is that robustness measured inside a tiny norm ball can be excellent while the model still fails on a modest rotation or a change of background. Unrestricted attacks are harder to optimise because there is no simple gradient path through the space of realistic images, so practitioners either search over parameterised transformations, paint smooth shapes onto the image in a spatially constrained way, or use a generative model to propose candidates that are then ranked by the target classifier. The last approach raises a subtle issue: if the generator itself is imperfect, failures may reflect unrealistic proposals rather than a genuine vulnerability. A well designed study therefore reports the perceptual quality of the generated inputs alongside the attack success rate. The value of unrestricted evaluation is that it tests the property we actually care about, which is whether the model's decision survives semantically irrelevant variation, rather than whether it survives a particular mathematical ball.""")
add("Targeted and Untargeted Attacks", """An untargeted attack succeeds as soon as the predicted class differs from the correct one, and it is normally the easiest to mount. A targeted attack must drive the model to one particular class chosen in advance, for example turning a stop sign into a speed limit sign rather than into any other object. The difference is not cosmetic. Untargeted attacks can exploit any nearby decision boundary, and decision boundaries in a high dimensional space are close to almost every point, so a single small step often suffices. Targeted attacks must move the input across several boundaries and into a specified region, which usually requires a larger budget, more iterations, or both. In practice the gap is large: a perturbation that reliably breaks untargeted classification at eight grey levels may achieve only a small targeted success rate at the same budget, and papers therefore report the two numbers separately. The choice also shapes defense evaluation. A defense that reduces untargeted success may still permit a determined attacker to reach a chosen class, and specific misclassifications can be far more damaging than generic errors, as when a face verification system is made to accept an impostor as a particular enrolled identity. Target selection itself is part of the adversary's planning, and the hardest and most informative target is often the class that is most similar in feature space, because it forces the attacker to cross a boundary that lies close to the original decision region.""")
add("Transferability Between Models", """A perturbation computed for one network frequently fools another network that was trained independently, even when the two use different architectures or different training sets. This property is called transferability, and it is the main reason black-box attacks are practical at all. The usual recipe is to train or obtain a substitute model, attack it in the white-box setting, and then submit the resulting inputs to the target. Transfer rates are far below white-box rates, commonly in the range of a few tens of percent for single step perturbations and higher for iterative ones, and they improve when the substitute is trained on data similar to the target's training distribution. Several explanations have been proposed. Different models trained on the same task tend to learn similar decision boundaries in the region of the data manifold, so gradients point in correlated directions, and perturbations that add energy along broadly shared features travel well. Larger perturbations, and perturbations found by averaging gradients over several models in an ensemble, transfer more reliably, though they are also more visible. Transferability cuts both ways for defenders: it means that protecting a deployed model requires evaluating it against attacks computed on unrelated models, and it means that publishing an attack does not require access to its intended victim. It also underlies the standard advice to use ensemble based evaluation when reporting robustness numbers.""")
add("Universal and Input Agnostic Perturbations", """Most adversarial perturbations are tailored to a single image, but it is also possible to compute one perturbation vector that, added to almost any image drawn from the data distribution, causes misclassification. These are called universal perturbations, and they show that vulnerability is a property of the model and the data distribution, not only of individual inputs. The standard construction starts from a small random vector and iteratively updates it so that a growing fraction of the training images is misclassified, projecting the vector back into the allowed norm ball after each step. A single vector often achieves high fooling rates across a large majority of a test set, and crucially it transfers to other models surprisingly well, which suggests that the directions it exploits correspond to shared properties of the learned representation rather than quirks of one network. Universal perturbations undermine a common intuition, namely that an attacker would have to craft a separate input for every victim. If one sticker like distortion reduces the reliability of an entire deployed system, the operational cost of an attack drops dramatically. They also interact with defenses in an informative way: adversarially trained models are markedly less susceptible to universal perturbations than standard models, and the residual fooling rate is a compact summary of the robustness of a whole model across a whole dataset.""")
add("Adversarial Examples in the Physical World", """Digital attacks assume the adversary controls the exact tensor that reaches the model. When the input comes from a camera, that assumption fails, and the attack must survive the full imaging pipeline: printing, a non frontal viewing angle, motion blur, sensor noise, compression and unknown illumination. Physical attacks handle this by optimising over a set of transformations and lighting conditions during the search, and by preferring perturbations that are robust in expectation rather than optimal for a single rendering. Two forms dominate the literature. Adversarial patches are localised, often high contrast patterns printed on a sticker and placed in the scene, for example next to a traffic sign or on a hat brim, and they work because they do not need to be subtle. Full image perturbations are also attempted by printing a photograph, but colour reproduction, paper reflectance and camera response typically destroy most of the fine grained structure that a small norm attack depends on, so the practical budget must be much larger than in the digital setting. Reported success rates must therefore be read together with the conditions of the experiment: distance, angle, lighting and printer calibration all matter, and a result obtained under a fixed frontal camera is much weaker evidence than one obtained while the object moves through a range of viewpoints. Physical evaluation is expensive, which is precisely why it is the setting where overconfident claims are most common.""")
add("Natural Adversarial Examples", """Not every failure of a classifier needs an attacker. Natural adversarial examples are images that were collected without any optimisation against a model yet cause confident errors, and they are valuable precisely because nobody crafted them. The usual methodology is to take a large pool of unlabelled photographs, run a well trained model over them, keep the cases where the model is confidently wrong, and then have human annotators verify the true label, which yields a dataset of hard but genuine images. The resulting errors differ from gradient based attacks in an important way. They are often caused by unusual pose, occlusion, texture bias or background context rather than by a dense, low amplitude perturbation that exists only to exploit a specific gradient. In fact they tend to be far from the original in pixel space while remaining unambiguous to a person. Their scientific role is to test whether robustness techniques generalise beyond the distribution they were trained against. A model that resists all small norm attacks can still fail on these images, which shows that adversarial training against one perturbation model does not automatically deliver perceptual robustness. For practitioners they serve as a practical benchmark with real stakes, since the failure modes they contain resemble the failures observed in deployment. They also remind us that the boundary between an adversarial and an ordinary mistake is a matter of intent and of construction method, not of the size of the error.""")
add("Evaluating Robustness Honestly", """A robustness claim is only as strong as the attack used to test it, so evaluation design decides whether a result means anything. Four rules cover most of the ground. First, report clean accuracy and adversarial accuracy together, at several perturbation budgets, and state the norm and radius explicitly, because a single number hides the trade off that matters most in deployment. Second, use attacks that are strong enough to matter, usually an iterative method such as projected gradient descent with multiple random restarts, and confirm that increasing the attack strength no longer changes the outcome, which is the practical definition of a converged attack. Third, evaluate against a suite that includes attacks the defense was not designed for, including transfer attacks from independently trained models. Fourth, present a few misclassified examples together with their perturbations, since visual inspection often reveals that an attack produced a texture the model could reasonably reject or that a claimed imperceptible perturbation is plainly visible. Two further cautions are worth stating. Averaging over a dataset can hide catastrophic failures on a small subset of classes, so per class breakdowns are informative. And accuracy under attack is not a probability of safety, because an adversary who needs to succeed only once is not constrained by an average over thousands of inputs.""")
add("Gradient Masking as a False Defense", """Some defenses appear robust because the attacker's optimiser cannot find a successful perturbation, not because no such perturbation exists. This failure mode is called gradient masking or obfuscated gradients, and it has invalidated a long series of published claims. Typical causes include numerical instability in the backward pass, where gradients become zero or take extreme values; stochastic preprocessing, so that the loss surface changes between evaluations; exploding or vanishing gradients caused by saturating nonlinearities; and randomised input transformations applied before classification. In each case a single step attack fails, and the reported robustness looks impressive, yet a slightly different method succeeds. Standard diagnostic tests are available. If a white-box attack performs worse than a simple transfer attack from another model, gradients are probably uninformative. If an attack does better with a larger perturbation budget, the search is not converging. If a random perturbation sometimes beats the optimised one, the optimiser is not working. If unrolling the stochastic parts of the model, or averaging gradients over several random draws, restores the attack, masking is confirmed. The remedy is methodological as much as technical: evaluate with adaptive attacks designed with full knowledge of the defense, use multiple attack families, and treat any unexplained gap between attack strength and reported robustness as a signal to investigate rather than a result to publish.""")
add("Randomized Smoothing and Certified Robustness", """Most defenses are empirical: they are tested against the attacks their authors could devise, and they may fail against an attack nobody has tried yet. Randomized smoothing offers something different, a guarantee. The construction wraps a base classifier in noise. To classify an input, the smoothed model draws many Gaussian perturbations, queries the base classifier on each, and returns the majority vote. The key theorem states that if the most likely class receives a probability of at least p under this noisy vote, then the prediction cannot change for any perturbation whose L two norm is smaller than a closed form radius that depends on p and on the noise level. The guarantee holds for any base classifier and any adversary, which is a genuinely stronger statement than any empirical result, and it scales to large networks because it does not require solving an optimisation problem at inference time. The price is visible in the formula. The certified radius grows only slowly as the confidence margin grows, and it shrinks as the input dimension rises, so the guaranteed radii on image benchmarks are small, typically a fraction of the budgets used in empirical attack studies. Certification also degrades clean accuracy, because the noise level must be tuned against the difficulty of the task, and building a base classifier that is accurate under heavy Gaussian noise is itself a substantial training problem.""")
add("Adversarial Training: The Enduring Baseline", """Adversarial training remains the most reliable empirical defense known. The idea is to change the training objective from minimising the loss on clean examples to minimising the worst case loss inside a perturbation ball, which turns learning into a min max problem. In practice the inner maximisation is approximated by an iterative attack that produces the training inputs, and the outer minimisation is ordinary stochastic gradient descent on those inputs, so the cost of training rises by roughly the number of attack steps. The approach was introduced alongside the fast gradient sign method and has survived a decade of attempts to replace it. Its weaknesses are also well documented. Robust models are usually less accurate on clean data at a fixed perturbation budget, and the trade off is not removable by simply training longer on larger models, though additional capacity and data do help. Robustness obtained this way is specific to the norm, the radius and the threat model used during training, so a model hardened against small L infinity changes may still fail on rotations or on a larger budget, and it may overfit to the particular attack used to generate the inner examples. Evaluation therefore has to use attacks stronger than the training attack, typically an iterative method with random restarts.""")
add("Adversarial Examples in Audio and Text", """Adversarial vulnerability is not restricted to images. In audio, an adversary adds a low amplitude waveform to a recording so that an automatic speech recognition system transcribes a different sentence while a human listener still hears the original, and the constraint is usually a bound on the L two or L infinity norm of the added signal. The difficulty is that the perturbation must survive playback through a loudspeaker and capture by a microphone as well as resampling and lossy compression, so studies optimise over a range of room responses and codecs. In text, gradient based methods do not apply directly because the input is discrete and small changes such as swapping a character or a synonym can alter meaning, so attacks search over substitutions that preserve grammar and semantics, scored by the target model, and they must often respect constraints such as keeping the modified token visually similar. Universality also appears in these domains: a single audio snippet can be mixed into many recordings, and a single phrase appended to many prompts can shift the behaviour of a language model. Across all of them the same conceptual structure holds, namely a threat model with a stated goal, a stated budget, and a stated measure of how much the change should be imperceptible to a human. What changes is the metric, since perceptual distance in audio depends on masking effects, and in text on meaning, not on any pixel norm.""")
add("Malware Evasion as an Adversarial Problem", """Malicious software detection is one of the few settings where adversarial machine learning has a direct operational meaning. A detector extracts features from a portable executable, such as the imported function names, the byte histogram, the section table or a learned embedding of the raw bytes, and a classifier decides whether the file is benign. The attacker's constraint is unusually strict: the modified file must still execute, must still perform its intended function, and must still parse correctly, so not every byte may be changed. Attackers work within this constraint by appending padding bytes, renaming sections, injecting dead code, changing the order of imports or restructuring the portable executable header, and by optimising the byte level changes with gradient information when a differentiable surrogate of the detector can be obtained. Two practical complications distinguish this domain. First, feedback is indirect, because an attacker learns whether a sample was flagged only by submitting it to a scanner or observing deployment, so query based attacks must be planned around limited and noisy signals. Second, the defender must control false positives on ordinary software, which severely limits how aggressive the classifier may be. Robustness evaluation here therefore combines malware family consistency with realistic functionality checks, and the standard warning applies: random byte noise is not a meaningful attack.""")
add("Detecting Adversarial Inputs", """Detecting adversarial inputs is often proposed as an alternative to making a classifier robust. The premise is that perturbed inputs differ statistically from clean ones, so a separate detector can flag them before they reach the model. Common signals include the size of the input gradient, since adversarial directions often have unusually large gradients or unusually small ones; the predictions of an ensemble, which may disagree more on perturbed inputs; the output distribution of the softmax, which is often more extreme for adversarial examples; and compression or quantisation of the input, since many attacks rely on fine grained variations that are destroyed by resizing or bit depth reduction. Some approaches reconstruct the input with an autoencoder and measure the reconstruction error. The weaknesses of the whole family are structural. A detector is itself a classifier, so it can be evaded by the same techniques that fool the main model, and its own gradient becomes part of the attacker's objective as soon as the adversary knows that it exists. Detection also does not repair anything, it only refuses service, which means false positives directly harm legitimate users and give the attacker a cheap denial of service channel. Most published detectors report strong results against the specific attack they were designed for and much weaker results under adaptive evaluation, so detection is best treated as one layer among several rather than as a solution.""")
add("Robust and Non-Robust Features", """One influential explanation of adversarial vulnerability begins with the observation that a dataset can contain features that are predictive but not meaningful. A small pattern of pixels that correlates with a class on the training distribution can be learned and used, even though it does not correspond to any property a human would recognise. Such features are called non robust, because a tiny perturbation aligned with them can change the class while leaving the semantic content of the image untouched. Robust features, by contrast, are those whose usefulness survives perturbation, and the hypothesis is that human perception relies mainly on this second kind. Under this view an adversarial example is not a strange artefact of neural networks but a direct result of training a model to maximise accuracy on a finite sample, which makes any predictive signal attractive, regardless of whether it is stable. The theory has empirical support. Datasets have been constructed by deliberately encoding only non robust features, and models trained on them are both accurate and highly vulnerable, and it is possible to train on adversarially perturbed data in a way that transfers robustness to a model trained on clean data. The account is not complete, since highly robust models still show errors and the separation between the two kinds of feature is not sharp, but it reframes the problem usefully, from an exotic attack to an ordinary consequence of the learning objective.""")
add("What a Threat Model Must Specify", """A threat model is the precise statement of what an adversary is assumed to be able to do, and without it a robustness claim has no content. At minimum it must name four things. The goal states what counts as a success, for example any change of the predicted label, a change to one particular class, or a reduction of accuracy over a whole dataset. The knowledge states what the adversary knows about the model, ranging from full access to the architecture, the weights and the training data to having only the ability to submit queries. The capabilities state the channels the adversary controls and the feedback it receives, for example reading the full probability vector, seeing only the top label, or receiving a numeric confidence score. The constraints state the budget, whether that is a norm bound on the perturbation, a limit on the number of queries, or a requirement that the modified input remain valid in a real system. Only after those choices are fixed does a sentence such as the model is robust at eight grey levels in the L infinity norm mean anything, because robustness is always robustness against something. A threat model is therefore not a preliminary formality but the definition of the problem being solved, and changing it silently changes the result.""")
add("The Adversary's Goal", """The adversary's goal is the first design decision in any threat model, and different goals lead to different attacks and different defenses. An untargeted goal asks only that the model be wrong, which is the weakest requirement and the easiest to satisfy. A targeted goal names the class the adversary wants, which is harder and is what matters when a specific outcome is valuable, as in making an impostor be accepted as a particular person. Some settings require a confidence threshold rather than a label change, so the adversary must push the score above or below a stated value. Others care about aggregate damage, such as reducing accuracy across a dataset by a specified amount, which is the natural goal for denial of service. The goal also determines what has to be preserved. A perturbation that changes the label is useless if it also breaks the input's validity, whether that means a file that no longer executes or a photograph that no longer looks like the object. Stating the goal precisely makes the attack measurable and prevents the common error of reporting a strong untargeted result as if it addressed a targeted concern, or of testing at a confidence level far removed from the one a deployed system actually uses as its decision threshold.""")
add("White-Box Adversaries", """A white-box adversary knows the model it is attacking: the architecture, the trained weights, and therefore the exact gradient of the loss with respect to the input. White-box access produces the strongest known attacks, and it is the appropriate assumption for two situations. The first is scientific, because a defense that has not been tested against a white-box adversary has not been tested against the strongest realistic attack, and any unexplained success is likely to be gradient masking. The second is practical, because models are frequently exposed through their weights, whether they are shipped to client devices, released openly, or reconstructed through model extraction, in which an attacker queries a service and trains a substitute until its behaviour matches. In this setting attacks are cheap: a single backward pass gives enough information for a fast gradient sign step, and a few dozen iterations of a projected gradient descent attack usually achieve near optimal success inside a small norm ball. The relevant defenses are consequently limited to those that change the geometry of the decision surface or add certified randomness, since hiding the gradient cannot help when the gradient is known. Reporting only black-box numbers for a model whose weights are public is a common and serious evaluation error.""")
